## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to apply public announcements to epistemic models and design a practical solver that scales with model size.

## Key Concepts
- Kripke models and accessibility relations
- Public announcements and product update
- Knowledge operator Ki and post-update evaluation
- Linear-time propagation and fixpoint reasoning

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal representation and product update sketch
function productUpdate(W, relations, V, pSet) {
  const Wp = W.filter(w => pSet.has(w));
  const Rp = {};
  for (const [AG, rel] of Object.entries(relations)) {
    Rp[AG] = new Map();
    for (const w of Wp) {
      Rp[AG].set(w, rel.get(w).filter(v => pSet.has(v)));
    }
  }
  return { W: Wp, relations: Rp };
}

function knows(pSet, w, agentRel) {
  const targets = agentRel.get(w) || [];
  return targets.every(u => pSet.has(u));
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How to extend to more agents or multi-modal knowledge (common knowledge)?
- How would you optimize for many sequential announcements or large models?
- What are potential numerical issues with non-deterministic updates in practice?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical logical deduction and simple SAT handling in a controlled, small-domain setting.

## Key Concepts
- Implication with negation
- Disjunction
- Satisfiability search
- Backtracking basics
- Early termination on first valid assignment

## Code Example
```javascript
function isSat(A,B) {
  // Clause1: (!A => B)  == (A || B)
  // Clause2: (B => A)   == (!B || A)
  // Clause3: (A || B)
  return (A || B) && (!B || A) && (A || B);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to three variables with similar clauses?
- How does short-circuit evaluation affect performance in larger clause sets?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of temporal, multi-step constraints in a 3-valued logic setting. Requires constructing a concrete model and outlining a backtracking propagator that preserves non-F constraints while allowing Unknowns.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued semantics for implication
- Temporal constraint propagation across time steps
- Unit/branching propagation in backtracking

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: evaluateImp(A,B) returns Kleene(A,B)
function evaluateImp(A,B){ // A,B in {T,F,U}
  // ¬A ∨ B with Kleene ordering T>U>F
  const not = {T:'F', F:'T', U:'U'}[A];
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  // ⩒ is max by value order
  const values = [not, B];
  let best = 'F';
  for (const v of values) if (order[v] > order[best]) best = v;
  return best;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a small 3-valued constraint solver and to enforce an exact-one-U constraint, a common pattern in policy engines and static analyzers.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic
- Constraint propagation
- Backtracking with pruning
- Exact-one constraint

## Code Example

```javascript
function satisfies(A,B,C){
  // A,B,C in {T,F,U}; returns true if all constraints not-F
  const not = v => v=== 'T' ? 'F' : v==='F' ? 'T' : 'U';
  const and = (x,y) => (x==='F'||y==='F')?'F':(x==='T'&&y==='T')?'T':'U';
  const or = (x,y) => (x==='T'||y==='T')?'T':(x==='F'&&y==='F')?'F':'U';
  const imp = (x,y) => not(x) || y;
  const c1 = imp(and(A,B), C);
  const c2 = imp(not(B), or(A,C));
  const c3 = imp(or(C, not(A)), B);
  return c1!=='F' && c2!=='F' && c3!=='F';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale to N variables with the exact-one-U constraint?
- What pruning strategies speed up the search in larger rule sets?

